afk: task subagents get their own eval kernel; sharing is opt-in.

Why: upstream task subagents inherit the parent's eval session id, so every
non-isolated subagent (same cwd) runs on the parent's Python kernel. With
bash disabled, that kernel is the command runner for all of them: one
agent's cell timeout interrupts or kills the kernel and destroys every
other agent's running cell and variables (observed 2026-10-04 nmm session:
five agents' cells died at the same instant, three times in five minutes;
agents saw NameErrors for their own definitions and clobbered each other's
globals).

What must survive a rebase:
- buildExecutorOptions passes parentEvalSessionId ONLY when the request
  sets shareEvalSession === true. No caller sets it, so task, workpool and
  eval-bridge children all fall back to their own session-file-keyed
  kernel. (Callers that already pass `shareEvalSession: false` are left
  untouched to keep the patch small.)
- The eval tool prompt no longer claims state persists across task
  subagents.
- Regression test in test/eval/agent-bridge.test.ts fails on the
  upstream default and passes with the patch.

